window.docContents[82].resolve({"/Automation/#Logical-Foundations--Automation___-More-Automation--Case-Study___-Regular-Expressions--The-___Weak___-Pumping-Lemma":{"contents":"One of the first really interesting theorems in the theory of\nregular expressions is the so-called pumping lemma, which\nstates, informally, that any sufficiently long string s matching\na regular expression re can be \"pumped\" by repeating some middle\nsection of s an arbitrary number of times to produce a new\nstring also matching re.  For the sake of simplicity, this\nexercise considers a slightly weaker theorem than is usually\nstated in courses on automata theory — hence the name\nweak_pumping.  The stronger one can be found below.\n\nTo get started, we need to define \"sufficiently long.\"  Since we\nare working in a constructive logic, we actually need to be able\nto calculate, for each regular expression re, a minimum length\nfor strings s to guarantee \"pumpability.\"\n\ndef pumpingConstant {α : Type} (re : RegExp α) : Nat :=\n  match re with\n  | EmptySet => 1\n  | EmptyStr => 1\n  | Char _ => 2\n  | App re₁ re₂ => re₁.pumpingConstant + re₂.pumpingConstant\n  | Union re₁ re₂ => re₁.pumpingConstant + re₂.pumpingConstant\n  | Star r => r.pumpingConstant\n\n\nYou may find these lemmas about the pumping constant useful when\nproving the pumping lemma below.\n\ntheorem pumping_constant_ge_1 {α : Type} (re : RegExp α) :\n    re.pumpingConstant ≥ 1 := by\n  induction re with (simp_all [pumpingConstant]; try lia)\n\ntheorem pumping_constant_0_false {α : Type} (re : RegExp α)\n    (h : re.pumpingConstant = 0) : False := by\n  have := pumping_constant_ge_1 re; lia\n\n\nNext, it is useful to define an auxiliary function that repeats a\nstring (appends it to itself) some number of times. Note\nhow we define simp lemmas for napp to go with its definition.\n\ndef napp {α : Type} (n : Nat) (l : List α) : List α :=\n  match n with\n  | 0 => []\n  | n' + 1 => l ++ napp n' l\n\n@[simp]\ntheorem napp_zero {α : Type} (l : List α) : napp 0 l = [] := by rfl\n\n@[simp]\ntheorem napp_succ {α : Type} (n : Nat) (l : List α) : napp (n + 1) l = l ++ napp n l := by rfl\n\n\nThese auxiliary lemmas might also be useful in your proof of the\npumping lemma.\n\n@[simp]\ntheorem napp_plus {α : Type} (n m : Nat) (l : List α) :\n    napp (n + m) l = napp n l ++ napp m l := by\n  induction n with simp_all [Nat.succ_add]\n\ntheorem napp_star {α : Type} (m : Nat) (s₁ s₂ : List α) (re : RegExp α)\n    (hs₁ : s₁ =~ re) (hs₂ : s₂ =~ Star re) :\n    napp m s₁ ++ s₂ =~ Star re := by\n  induction m with\n  | zero => simp only [napp_zero, List.nil_append]; trivial\n  | succ m ih =>\n    simp only [napp_succ]\n    rw [List.append_assoc]\n    apply mStarApp <;> trivial\n\n\nThe (weak) pumping lemma itself says that, if s =~ re and if the\nlength of s is at least the pumping constant of re, then s\ncan be split into three substrings s₁ ++ s₂ ++ s₃ in such a way\nthat s₂ can be repeated any number of times and the result, when\ncombined with s₁ and s₃, will still match re.\nSince s₂ is also guaranteed not to be the empty string, this gives us\na (constructive!) way to generate strings matching re that are\nas long as we like.\n\nThis proof is quite long, so to make it more tractable we've\nbroken it up into a number of subproofs, which we then assemble\nto prove the main lemma.\n\nYour job is to complete the proofs of the helper lemmas; the main\nlemma relies on these.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Automation: More Automation\u0009Case Study: Regular Expressions","header":"10.5.4. The \"Weak\" Pumping Lemma","id":"/Automation/#Logical-Foundations--Automation___-More-Automation--Case-Study___-Regular-Expressions--The-___Weak___-Pumping-Lemma"},"/Poly/#Logical-Foundations--Poly___-Polymorphism-and-Higher-Order-Functions--Polymorphism":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Poly: Polymorphism and Higher-Order Functions","header":"6.1. Polymorphism","id":"/Poly/#Logical-Foundations--Poly___-Polymorphism-and-Higher-Order-Functions--Polymorphism"},"/Preface/#Logical-Foundations--Preface--For-Potential-Contributors":{"contents":"If you find things you'd like to help add or improve, your\ncontributions are welcome!  To get started, clone the\nSF-in-Lean git repo and\nhave a look at ALPHA-TESTERS.md.\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Preface","header":"1.4. For Potential Contributors","id":"/Preface/#Logical-Foundations--Preface--For-Potential-Contributors"}});